the European Union's cohesion policy  investing in your regions and cities the  economic and social situation is very  different in each of the European  Union's 274 regions the main objective  of the EU reformed cohesion policy is to  reduce these differences by promoting  economic growth job creation and  competitiveness over the past five years  EU cohesion policy has created over  600,000 jobs invested in the  professional training of up to 15  million people every year to improve  their employability and co-financed the  construction of 25 thousand kilometres  of roads and 1,800 kilometers of  Railways it's also ensured the provision  of clean drinking water for three  million more people has invested in two  hundred thousand small and medium-sized  companies and sixty one thousand  research projects these figures are  still growing as projects which started  over the past five years continue to  produce results all this is made  possible by the European Regional  Development Fund the cohesion fund and  the European Social Fund which together  represent more than one third of the EU  budget  these cohesion policy investments are in  turn coordinated with the European  agricultural fund for Rural Development  and the European maritime and Fisheries  fund and complement other sources of EU  funding the EU cohesion policy is  reviewed by the EU institutions every 7  years before being implemented by the  Member States in their regions the  latest review of the policy concluded in  2013 and implemented as from 2014 is  designed to help achieve the Europe 2020  strategy objectives of smart sustainable  and inclusive growth from 2014 to 2020  cohesion policy investments will  prioritize fewer key areas these include  research and innovation the Digital  Agenda support for SMEs the low-carbon  economy employment and mobility better  education and better public  administration the new policy will  invest even more in people by  reinforcing the European Social Fund and  by placing greater emphasis on social  inclusion and tackling youth  unemployment they will also become  easier for regions from different  countries to work together across  borders with increased funding for  territorial cooperation to ensure money  is spent wisely cohesion policy now  focuses even more on objectives and  results with greater emphasis on  evaluation and incentives for effective  projects through simplified procedures  and harmonized rules  the revised cohesion policy aims at  bringing out the best in every region  making them more competitive and  boosting economic growth and job  creation communicating the results of EU  cohesion investments is essential so  that successful projects can be  recognised and European citizens can see  exact  how their money is being spent  you
 

